{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788304369,"nonce":"gv4ZjFQpfpBx8QqtBLq7GJYPS7w=","message":"TWO OF THE FOUR ACCEPTANCE FAILURES ARE THIS ISSUE'S, AND THEY LAND ON OPPOSITE SIDES. Branch `fix/s21-suite-failures`, commits `a6b6777` (mod defect + suite) and `8d17ccf` (suite only), off `2bf4539`. **Nothing below has met a bench** — the source change builds clean in Release and the branch carries no assembly.\n\nLog: `accept/runs/s21-20260901/suites/61794cd.log`, 290 PASS / 4 FAIL / 0 SKIP of 294.\n\n## 7.2h — MOD DEFECT. `enabled_but_incapable` was emitted only when it was non-empty\n\n    7.2h  FAIL  `work-cover` publishes data.still_under.0.enabled_but_incapable\n          expected: the key to be present\n          actual:   null\n\n`work-cover`'s `still_under` row is built with eleven unconditional keys and one conditional one:\n\n    if (r.Impaired.Count \u003e 0) { … why[\"enabled_but_incapable\"] = imp; }\n\n`WorkCoverage.Section`'s under-row has the identical guard. Phase 7 stages the M1 end-state by turning Doctor OFF across the whole roster (`set_doctor(ids, 0)`), so `r.Enabled` is empty, `r.Impaired` is empty by construction — `Compute` only reaches `Impaired` for a pawn already in `Enabled` — and the key was simply absent. **Not \"wrong dig path\", not \"wrong fixture\": the field does not exist in that state.**\n\n**Ruled a defect, and fixed.** An absent key meant BOTH \"nobody enabled here is missing a capacity\" AND \"this build does not publish that key\". That is the conflation `61794cd` already ruled against for `ticks_until_bleedout` (null, never omitted, never `int.MaxValue`), the one `PawnActs.NoStamp()` exists for, and the one `triage` cites in its own source (\"Present and null, so a caller can prove the verb mutated nothing rather than infer it\"). Every sibling in the same dictionary — `available_pawns`, `candidates` — is already an always-present, possibly empty list. It is now one too, in both homes.\n\nComment #2's contract sentence survives with one word added: `work-cover`'s note now says a **non-empty** `enabled_but_incapable` list means the fix is surgery, and `checklists/triggered.md` item 7 says to branch on whether the list has entries rather than on whether it is there.\n\n**FINDING 4.7 is NOT the same defect and stays open.** The orchestrator's brief suspected \"the same mistake in a second place\" and it is half right: the INNER guard `if (r.Impaired.Count \u003e 0)` was in two places and both are fixed. The OUTER `if (r.Under)` that 4.7 names is a different thing — it is what keeps a FINE row to three fields, it is the digest's stated byte budget, and check 3.4a asserts it as a set. Whether a COVERED row should carry the diagnosis at all is a design decision, not a shape bug, and I have not made it from the chair.\n\nNew checks: **3.3q** (the list is EMPTY rather than absent when nobody is impaired — phase 3 has exactly one colonist with Doctor enabled and 3.3e says that one is available, so `Impaired` is arithmetically empty), **7.2i** (the impaired are a subset of the enabled), **9.8d** and **9.11a** (re-derived from the source in both files, because a live fixture WITH an impaired pawn passes either way — which is precisely how the same omission survived into a second file unnoticed). `enabled_but_incapable` joins `UNDER_ROW_KEYS`, so 3.3a's set comparison no longer subtracts it.\n\n## 6.4c / 6.4d — SUITE BUG, and the mod is right in a way worth writing down\n\n    6.4c  FAIL  …and a refusal names `no-bed`, the last clause of TakeToBedGate\n          actual: [{\"pawn\":574,\"gate\":\"manipulation\"},{\"pawn\":578,\"gate\":\"manipulation\"}]\n\nThe suite read the gate off `casualties[0]`, which this fixture makes **the standing bleeder**. `TakeToBedGate(\"rescue\", …)` opens on `HealthAIUtility.CanRescueNow` → `WantsToBeRescued`, whose FIRST clause is `!pawn.Downed`; `RestUtility.FindBedFor` is its LAST. **So `no-bed` is unreachable by construction for a standing patient** — every candidate that clears `ProviderGate` reads `cannot-rescue` instead, and no amount of staging changes that. `TriageVerbs.cs` says so in its own header (\"THE OTHER BOUNDARY, STATED\").\n\nThe two `manipulation` rows are the handless doctor and the anaesthetised patient, both refused by `ProviderGate` before the bed lookup is reached. **That is not fixture leakage.** Check 6.8d deliberately REQUIRES phase 4's handless doctor to be in the gated list; the wreckage crosses phases on purpose and the suite's own header says so. The orchestrator's fixture-isolation hypothesis is refuted: the isolation was never claimed for this pawn, and repairing it would break 6.8d.\n\nFixed as a suite bug: 6.4a now asserts `no-rescuer` across EVERY casualty (which is what its own sentence always claimed and it was only checking the first row), **6.4b2** asserts the standing half by name — `cannot-rescue` present, `no-bed` absent — and 6.4c/6.4d select the row by `downed` rather than by index. The banked envelope `18-triage-downed.json` is that row and 9.6j already asserted it offline, which is exactly why the offline half of the suite was green while the live half was red.\n\n`bed_count()` and `spawn_bed()`'s own docstrings said \"with NO bed on the map every candidate is refused `no-bed`\" and now say which refusal belongs to which patient. Recorded in DESIGN's decisions log, including a correction to the 2026-09-01 entry above it, which repeats the same overclaim.\n\n## The third un-filed finding is now an issue: `6fc75e3`\n\nFINDING 5.2c — a clean `work-cover {dry_run:true}` emits nothing, correctly, and then reports it through `Stamp(0)`, whose sentence is *\"NOT WRITTEN — the journal writer is closed … treat anything done in this session as unprovenanced.\"* Neither clause is true. Filed with an Acceptance section, including the part that matters: the discriminator must be the emit GUARD, not the returned seq, because `Journal.Emit` really can return 0 when the writer really is closed and that case must keep its warning. Not fixed on this branch — it is a separate defect with its own acceptance.\n\n3.7 (`9b179ef`) and the dry-run `coverage_after` (`58794e4`) are untouched and remain findings.\n\n## What the orchestrator must re-run, and what to expect\n\n**The source fix needs a Release rebuild before the re-run** — 7.2h, 7.2i, 3.3q, 3.3a and 9.8d/9.11a all assert the new shape and will fail against the `1.0.0+52606d1` artifact currently on the bench. The suite-only fixes (6.4, phase 7b) do not.\n\n`./accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.py` full sweep on a **fresh `--quicktest` map with no beds** (the suite wrecks its colony; reload before re-running). Expected: 6.4c/6.4d PASS on the anaesthetised patient's row with `gate:\"no-bed\"` and the game's own `No reachable, un-reserved non-prisoner bed…` sentence; new 6.4b2 PASS with `cannot-rescue` on the bleeder; 7.2h PASS with `[]`; 3.3q PASS with `[]`.","files":null}]}